263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 226 0 R
/T (1. In the TruckDrive clips, LiDAR provided more information about vehicles at a distance. Across five scenes, out of 504 observations, the labelled vehicles had LiDAR returns in 80.36% of the cases, compared with 37.50% for radar, and in 31 out of 36 observations \(radar: 12\) in two additional clips. This suggests that LiDAR could be used to obtain early information before a truck needs to respond; however, it is still necessary to measure both the stability of recognition and stopping performance. One caveat is that on TruckScenes, when LiDAR is thinned to the same point count as the radar, its ability to detect vehicles beyond 50 m disappears. This kind of control has not been carried out on TruckDrive \(Sections 4.2 and 5\). )
/E (1. In the TruckDrive clips, LiDAR provided more information about vehicles at a distance. Across five scenes, out of 504 observations, the labelled vehicles had LiDAR returns in 80.36% of the cases, compared with 37.50% for radar, and in 31 out of 36 observations \(radar: 12\) in two additional clips. This suggests that LiDAR could be used to obtain early information before a truck needs to respond; however, it is still necessary to measure both the stability of recognition and stopping performance. One caveat is that on TruckScenes, when LiDAR is thinned to the same point count as the radar, its ability to detect vehicles beyond 50 m disappears. This kind of control has not been carried out on TruckDrive \(Sections 4.2 and 5\). )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 226 0 R
/T (2. The radar did not provide complete coverage of all important targets. In TruckScenes, for instance, it registered signals in 99.12% of truck observations, 67.66% of observations involving adult pedestrians and 39.00% of those involving traffic cones. Since the samples for the different classes are not the same, these figures represent coverage observations and not evidence of an effect )
/E (2. The radar did not provide complete coverage of all important targets. In TruckScenes, for instance, it registered signals in 99.12% of truck observations, 67.66% of observations involving adult pedestrians and 39.00% of those involving traffic cones. Since the samples for the different classes are not the same, these figures represent coverage observations and not evidence of an effect )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
